 -1630s1960s-with-clarissa-von-spee-curator-cleveland-museum-of-art
DESCRIPTION:https://events.stanford.edu/event/print-revolutions-chinese-imp
 ressions-1630s1960s-with-clarissa-von-spee-curator-cleveland-museum-of-art
 \n\nIn August 2025\, the Cleveland Museum of Art (CMA) and the Metropolita
 n Museum of Art (the Met) announced the acquisition of well over 200 rare\
 , 18th century Suzhou woodblock prints from the collection of Christer von
  de Burg. The purchase brings some of the most sophisticated Chinese color
  prints to the United States\, enabling both institutions to tell the stor
 y of printmaking\, in combination with their world-class painting collecti
 ons\, in new ways and innovative displays.\n\nThe talk will introduce the 
 exhibition Print Revolutions: Chinese Impressions\, 1630s–1960s at the C
 MA (15 November\, 2026 - 4 April\, 2027)\, showcasing some of the finest e
 xemplars of Chinese printmaking\, many of them seen in public for the firs
 t time. More than eighty prints\, paintings\, and porcelains from the muse
 um’s collection\, as well as a few loans\, illustrate the development of
  color printing from the 1600s leading up to the international modern wood
 cut movement in the early 1900s.  \n\nClarissa von Spee\, PhD\, is Chair o
 f the Department of Asian Art and James and Donna Reid Curator of Chinese 
 Art\, as well as Interim Curator of Islamic Art at the Cleveland Museum of
  Art. \n\nThis event is co-sponsored by the Department of Art & Art Histor
 y and the Center for East Asian Studies.\n\nImage: Gusu Beauty–Mother an
 d Son in a Summer Pavilion (姑蘇美人 – 夏日亭畔母子圖)\, 1736
 –95. Southeast China\, Suzhou Qing dynasty (1644–1911)\, Qianlong peri
 od (1736–95). Polychrome woodblock print\; ink and color on paper\; prin
 t only: 104.2 x 
